      "text": "Given the line passes through the points $(2,2,1)$ and $(5,1,-2),$ we find a direction vector of the line by subtracting the coordinates of these points:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\vec{d} &= (5,1,-2) - (2,2,1) \\\\\n&= (3,-1,-3).\n\\end{align*}\n\nThe general form of the line's equation is given by:\n\\[\n(x,y,z) = (2,2,1) + k(3,-1,-3),\n\\]\nwhere $k$ is the scalar parameter.  \n\nWe are given a point on the line with an $x$-coordinate of 4.  The $x$-coordinate is given by:\n\\[\nx = 2 + 3k.\n\\]\nSetting $x$ equal to 4, we find:\n\\begin{align*}\n4 &= 2 + 3k \\\\\n4 - 2 &= 3k \\\\\n2 &= 3k \\\\\nk &= \\frac{2}{3}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nSubstituting this value of $k$ back into a parameterization of the line gives $z$-coordinate:\n\\begin{align*}\nz &= 1 - k - 3k \\\\\n &= 1 - \\frac{2}{3} - \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n &= 1 - \\frac{4}{3} \\\\\n &= -\\frac{1}{3}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nThus, the $z$-coordinate of the point is $\\boxed{-\\frac{1}{3}}$.",
