      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we will first find the direction vector of the line that passes through the points $(2,2,1)$ and $(5,1,-2)$. The direction vector is the difference between the coordinates of the two points.\n\nThe direction vector is given by:\n\\[ \\begin{pmatrix} 5 - 2 \\\\ 1 - 2 \\\\ -2 - 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\nThe parametric equations of the line can be written as:\n\\[ \\begin{cases} x = 2 + 3t \\\\ y = 2 - t \\\\ z = 1 - 3t \\end{cases} \\]\n\nGiven that a point on this line has an $x$-coordinate of 4, we can substitute $x = 4$ and solve for $t$:\n\\[ 4 = 2 + 3t \\implies 2 = 3t \\implies t = \\frac{2}{3} \\]\n\nNow substitute $t = \\frac{2}{3}$ into the $z$-coordinate equation:\n\\[ z = 1 - 3\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) = 1 - 2 = -1 \\]\n\nThus, the $z$-coordinate of the point on the line with an $x$-coordinate of 4 is $-1$.\n\nWe can confirm this using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Given points and finding the direction vector\nx1, y1, z1 = 2, 2, 1\nx2, y2, z2 = 5, 1, -2\n\ndirection_vector = [x2 - x1, y2 - y1, z2 - z1]\nprint(direction_vector)\n```\n```output\n[3, -1, -3]\n```\nThe direction vector of the line is correctly calculated as $\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}$. This means that for every change of $t = 1$ in the $x$, $y$, and $z$ coordinates respectively, we move $3$ units in the $x$ direction, $-1$ unit in the $y$ direction, and $-3$ units in the $z$ direction.\n\nGiven that one point on the line has coordinates $(4, 2, 1)$ and the line passes through $(2, 2, 1)$, the $x$-coordinate changes from $2$ to $4$, a change of $2$. Since $t$ represents this change, we solve for $t$:\n\\[ t = \\frac{4 - 2}{3} = \\frac{2}{3} \\]\n\nNow we substitute $t = \\frac{2}{3}$ into the $z$-coordinate equation:\n\\[\nz = 1 - 3\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) = 1 - 2 = -1\n\\]\n\nHence, the $z$-coordinate of the point is $\\boxed{-1}$.",
